`,s.jsx(G,{items:[{q:"A kernel's weights sum to 0. What kind of filter is it, and what does its output look like on a flat wall?",a:"It is a derivative filter — an edge detector. On a flat region every neighbour is equal, so the weighted sum is (constant) times (sum of weights) = 0. The output is exactly zero there, which is why derivative outputs are displayed with 128 as the zero point: the responses are signed."},{q:"Why does a median filter beat a Gaussian on salt-and-pepper noise?",a:"Because a 0 or a 255 impulse is an extreme value, and the median of nine values can never be an extreme unless most of the window is corrupted. The Gaussian averages the impulse in, so it survives as a dimmer, wider smudge — and the averaging blurs the real edges at the same time. Median is a rank statistic, not a weighted sum, so it is non-linear and cannot be expressed as a kernel."},{q:"How many multiply-adds per pixel does a separable 11x11 Gaussian need, versus the naive version?",a:"22 versus 121 — two 1-D passes of 11 taps each instead of one 2-D pass of 121 taps, a 5.5x saving. It works because the 2-D Gaussian matrix is rank 1, the outer product of a 1-D Gaussian with itself, and convolution is associative."},{q:"Name the step in Canny that reappears almost unchanged in object detection.",a:"Non-maximum suppression. Canny keeps a pixel only if it is the maximum along the gradient direction, thinning a thick edge to a single line. Detectors keep a box only if it is the highest-scoring one among heavily overlapping candidates, collapsing a cluster of detections to one. Same greedy local-winner rule, different neighbourhood definition."}]}),`
